    "code": "",
    "options": {
      "A": "In `model.eval()`, both `Dropout` and `BatchNorm2d` are completely bypassed as identity functions.",
      "B": "In `model.train()`, `BatchNorm2d` uses fixed running statistics, whereas in `model.eval()` it computes per-batch mean and variance.",
      "C": "In `model.eval()`, `Dropout` becomes an identity pass-through (dropping 0% of elements without scaling), and `BatchNorm2d` normalizes inputs using its learned running mean and running variance instead of the current mini-batch statistics.",
      "D": "In `model.eval()`, `Dropout` multiplies all activations by `0.5` and `BatchNorm2d` continues updating its running mean and variance from the test batches."
    },
    "correct": [
      "C"
    ],
    "explanation": "PyTorch uses *inverted dropout*: during `model.train()`, `nn.Dropout(p)` randomly zeroes elements with probability $p$ and scales remaining elements by $\\frac{1}{1-p}$, so that during `model.eval()`, `Dropout` is a pure identity function. For `nn.BatchNorm2d`, `model.train()` normalizes using current batch statistics and updates `running_mean`/`running_var`, whereas `model.eval()` freezes and uses the accumulated `running_mean` and `running_var`.",
    "option_explanations": {
      "A": "Incorrect. `BatchNorm2d` is NOT bypassed in `eval()`; it still normalizes using `running_mean`/`running_var` and applies learned affine parameters $\\gamma, \\beta$.",
      "B": "Incorrect. This reverses training and evaluation behavior for `BatchNorm2d`.",
      "C": "Correct. Inverted dropout makes `eval()` an identity operation, and `BatchNorm2d` switches from mini-batch statistics to frozen running statistics.",
      "D": "Incorrect. Because PyTorch scales by $1/(1-p)$ during *training*, no `0.5` multiplication is needed in `eval()`, and `BatchNorm2d` freezes running stats in `eval()`."
    },
    "competency": "Contrast Dropout and BatchNorm behavior between model.train() and model.eval()",
