      "A": "Incorrect because `backward()` is deterministic; identical weights produce identical activations and identical gradients, so symmetry is never broken.",
      "B": "Correct. Zero initialization fails to break symmetry (and causes zero gradients into earlier layers when downstream weights are zero), preventing hidden units from differentiating.",
      "C": "Incorrect because PyTorch allows zero-valued parameters and does not raise an exception during `optimizer.step()`.",
      "D": "Incorrect because deterministic zero gradients do not produce `NaN`; instead, weights remain stuck or locked in symmetry."
