    "stem": "What is the performance benefit of calling `optimizer.zero_grad(set_to_none=True)` (which became the default in recent PyTorch versions) compared to `optimizer.zero_grad(set_to_none=False)`?",
    "code": "optimizer.zero_grad(set_to_none=True)",
    "options": {
      "A": "It freezes all parameters by setting `param.requires_grad = False`.",
      "B": "Instead of launching a `memset` / zero-fill kernel to write `0.0` into every element of `param.grad`, it sets `param.grad = None`, avoiding memory writes during `zero_grad()` and allowing the subsequent `backward()` pass to assign the gradient directly instead of executing a read-modify-write addition (`0.0 + grad`).",
      "C": "It deletes the parameter tensors `param.data` to save GPU memory between epochs.",
      "D": "It skips `loss.backward()` for layers whose gradients are small."
    },
    "correct": [
      "B"
    ],
    "explanation": "With `set_to_none=False`, `zero_grad()` fills every `.grad` tensor with zeros in memory, and the next `backward()` adds the newly computed gradient to that zero tensor (`grad += new_grad`). With `set_to_none=True`, `param.grad` is simply set to `None` (no memory write), and `backward()` directly allocates/assigns the gradient without an extra addition kernel, improving both speed and peak memory efficiency.",
    "option_explanations": {
      "A": "Incorrect because `param.requires_grad` remains `True`.",
      "B": "Correct. `set_to_none=True` avoids zero-filling memory and replaces `0 + grad` accumulation on the first backward pass with direct assignment.",
      "C": "Incorrect because `zero_grad` only affects `param.grad`, never `param.data`.",
      "D": "Incorrect because `backward()` still computes exact gradients for all parameters with `requires_grad=True`."
    },
