    "stem": "In PyTorch's `torch.utils.data` architecture, why are data primitives (`Dataset` and `DataLoader`) explicitly separated from the `nn.Module` model definition and training loop?",
    "code": "",
    "options": {
      "A": "To decouple data storage, preprocessing, and minibatch iteration from model training logic for better readability, modularity, and maintainability.",
      "B": "Because `nn.Module` cannot accept raw tensors directly and requires a `DataLoader` wrapper to invoke `.forward()`.",
      "C": "Because PyTorch's autograd engine only tracks gradients on tensors yielded by a `torch.utils.data.Dataset` subclass.",
      "D": "To force all datasets to be loaded entirely into GPU VRAM prior to initializing model weights."
    },
    "correct": [
      "A"
    ],
    "explanation": "As highlighted in Module 4 (`pytorch_data_utilities.ipynb`), code for processing data samples can get messy and hard to maintain; PyTorch provides `Dataset` (which stores/retrieves individual samples and labels) and `DataLoader` (which wraps an iterable around the `Dataset` for batching, shuffling, and multiprocessing) to decouple dataset code from model training code.",
    "option_explanations": {
      "A": "Correct. Decoupling data handling from training code improves modularity, readability, and reusability across experiments.",
      "B": "Incorrect. `nn.Module` accepts any appropriately shaped `torch.Tensor` directly regardless of whether it came from a `DataLoader`.",
      "C": "Incorrect. Autograd tracks operations on any tensor with `requires_grad=True`, independent of `Dataset`.",
      "D": "Incorrect. `Dataset` typically loads samples lazily on the CPU rather than preloading the entire dataset into GPU memory."
    },
    "competency": "Explain the architectural role of Dataset and DataLoader in decoupling data pipelines from model code",
    "source": "modules/module_4_working_with_data/pytorch_data_utilities.ipynb"
