    "difficulty": "Medium",
    "multi_select": false,
    "stem": "In `torchvision.transforms`, why does passing `transforms.RandomErasing(p=0.5)` BEFORE `transforms.ToTensor()` in `transforms.Compose` raise a `TypeError`?",
    "code": "from torchvision import transforms\n\ntf = transforms.Compose([\n    transforms.RandomHorizontalFlip(p=0.5),\n    transforms.RandomErasing(p=0.5),\n    transforms.ToTensor()\n])",
    "options": {
      "A": "Because `p=0.5` is invalid; `RandomErasing` requires an integer percentage `p=50`.",
      "B": "Because `RandomErasing` cannot be combined with `RandomHorizontalFlip` in the same pipeline.",
      "C": "Because `RandomErasing` requires a 4D batch tensor and can only be run inside `collate_fn`.",
      "D": "Because `RandomErasing` is a Tensor-only transform (like `Normalize`) and cannot operate on a PIL Image; it must be placed *after* `ToTensor()`."
    },
    "correct": [
      "D"
    ],
    "explanation": "In classic `torchvision.transforms`, most geometric/color transforms (`Resize`, `RandomCrop`, `RandomHorizontalFlip`, `ColorJitter`) accept either PIL Images or Tensor images, whereas `Normalize` and `RandomErasing` operate *strictly* on `torch.Tensor` images (`(C, H, W)` or `(B, C, H, W)`) and must be placed after `ToTensor()`.",
    "option_explanations": {
      "A": "Incorrect. Probability `p` in `torchvision.transforms` is a float in `[0.0, 1.0]`.",
      "B": "Incorrect. `RandomErasing` and `RandomHorizontalFlip` are routinely used together when ordered properly.",
      "C": "Incorrect. `RandomErasing` operates on individual `(C, H, W)` image tensors after `ToTensor()`.",
      "D": "Correct. `RandomErasing` expects a `torch.Tensor` image and fails with `TypeError` if given a PIL Image prior to `ToTensor()`."
    },
    "competency": "Distinguish PIL-compatible transforms from Tensor-only transforms (Normalize, RandomErasing)",
    "source": "LFD2001 Ch. 4"
